  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\n  The Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem involves finding a step function h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1] with integral 1 that minimizes  \n  C\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx.  \n  This problem is challenging due to its non-convexity and high-dimensional solution space (even for n=70).  \n  We adopt a hierarchical approach that balances exploration and exploitation, combining coarse-grained initialization with fine-grained local search.  \n  The method starts with a coarser grid (n\u2081 * m\u2081 + n\u2082 * m\u2082 + ...) and gradually increases resolution, projecting the solution upward.  \n  This approach leverages the intuition that a low-resolution solution serves as a guiding structure for higher-resolution search, improving efficiency and robustness. Gradient-based optimization is used in each resolution level, with probabilistic repair mechanisms to maintain feasibility.\n\n  1. Coarse-to-fine resolution by structured grid generation (e.g., n=4, 15, 70) and projection\n  2. Stochastic gradient descent (via LBFGS) for optimization in each grid\n  3. Multi-start local search using perturbed solutions\n  4. Hybrid of continuous and combinatorial approaches: converts continuous solution to ternary representation for faster repairs\n  \n  Mathematical Specification:\n  - Representation:  \n    h \u2208 [0,1]\u207f, \u2211h = N/2 (where N=n_points, dx=2/N).  \n    Uses both continuous and ternary representations for optimization:  \n      Ternary: h[i] \u2208 { -\u03b1, 0, \u03b1 } for some \u03b1 \u2208 [0, 0.5], with integral preservation via compensatory constraints.\n  - Objective: Minimize C\u2085(h) = dx * max\u2096 (np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\"))\n  - Constraints:  \n    \u2022 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1\u2082\u2083 (briefly)  \n    \u2022 \u2211h[i] = N/2 (enforced via projected gradients)\n\n  Implementation Procedure:  \n  These codes are expressed as a step-by-step operational sequence with dimension handling and interfaces explicitly.\n\n  1. Preprocessing (Once at start):\n     1.1. Monitor budget timer (core)\n     1.2. Identify available initial parent if provided\n     1.3. Resolve grid configurations (default: [4,15,70]) and allocate storage\n\n  2. Lowest Grid (n=4):\n     2.1. Initialize candidate(s) with provided parent or uniform random with penalty-defined feasibility\n     2.2. Feasibility check: clip + scale to enforce [0,1] and integral constraint\n     2.3. Objective = dx\u2084 * np.correlate(h,1-h,m=\"full\").max()\n     2.4. Stochastic Gradient Descent (line searches, LBFGS) \u2192 candidate solutions\n\n  3. Medium Grid (n=15):\n     3.1. Project previous best solution via nearest-neighbor expansion + ternary repair\n     3.2. Apply local search (SLSQP) with black-box term constraints\n     3.3. Maintain feasibility via probabilistic component repair\n\n  4. Highest Grid (n=70):\n     4.1. Project candidate from n=15 to n=70 using piecewise interpolation\n     4.2. Enhanced local search (ADAM with Nesterov momentum)\n     4.3. Apply multi-stage repair if out-of-bounds occur\n\n  5. Verification & Termination:\n     5.1. Maintain history of C\u2085 across stages\n     5.2. Break on grid completion or budget exhaustion\n     5.3. Fallback to last-projected solution if no new better found\n\n  6. Candidate Evaluation:\n     6.1. Non-cached correlation-based score computation\n     6.2. Feasibility enforced though compensatory constraints (e.g., \u2211h[i] projected back)\n     6.3. Solution repair if violated (LBFGS repair implies gradient knowledge)\n\n  Search Choices and Variants:\n  Default policy:\n      Active grids: [4, 15, 70]\n      Projection method: Cubic spline interpolation\n      Repair threshold: 5% violation triggers repair\n      Non-linear restarts: if plateau found, re-initialize grid with random noise\n      Time budget splitting: allocate 20%, 40%, 40% of budget to each grid\n      \n  Compute Budget and Robustness:\n      ESTIMATED TIME BREAKDOWN:\n        \u2022 Low grid: 100s (assumes 1.2k evals \u00d7 0.1s eval)\n        \u2022 Medium grid: 600s (3k evals \u00d7 0.2s eval)\n        \u2022 High grid: 400s (2.2k evals \u00d7 0.2s eval)\n      Fallback tolerant: grid jumps give at least factorial improvement factor\n      BOUND PROTECTION: Value restoration by ternary projection or grid-independent repair\n\n  Validation and Return Contract:\n      - Input validation ensures seed/policy integration\n      - Output enforced: \n        0 \u2264 exact_component_prob_and_integral \n        sum(h) = N/2\n      - Score validated via exact code duplication\n      - Final validation: snapshot check of result vs. provided metrics",
